1
00:00:00,731 --> 00:00:05,302
On October 4, 1997,

2
00:00:05,302 --> 00:00:11,441
I walked into a room unlike any room I'd ever entered before.

3
00:00:11,441 --> 00:00:16,613
People were standing up, sharing their stories.

4
00:00:16,613 --> 00:00:22,619
A wave of relief washed over me.

5
00:00:22,619 --> 00:00:25,689
I got a cup of coffee

6
00:00:25,689 --> 00:00:29,927
and just took everything in.

7
00:00:29,927 --> 00:00:31,762
At that point,

8
00:00:31,762 --> 00:00:36,466
I was at the lowest point in my life.

9
00:00:36,466 --> 00:00:38,669
I was out of ideas,

10
00:00:38,669 --> 00:00:44,775
and I didn't know how I was going to make it out of the condition I was in.

11
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:49,379
Before that date, my life had not been easy.

12
00:00:49,379 --> 00:00:53,750
When I was 25 years old, I gave birth to a son.

13
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:58,922
We called him K.K.

14
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:03,026
The next thing that happened changed my life forever.

15
00:01:03,026 --> 00:01:06,029
He was struck by a car.

16
00:01:06,029 --> 00:01:09,232
That is the day I lost him.

17
00:01:09,232 --> 00:01:12,002
At only five years old,

18
00:01:12,002 --> 00:01:16,173
he was gone.

19
00:01:16,173 --> 00:01:21,011
His laughter, his love, his energy

20
00:01:21,011 --> 00:01:27,884
were all taken from me.

21
00:01:27,884 --> 00:01:33,624
My life became a downward spiral.

22
00:01:33,624 --> 00:01:36,493
I began to drink.

23
00:01:36,493 --> 00:01:40,197
Drinking led to substance use.

24
00:01:40,197 --> 00:01:46,803
For the substance use, I was imprisoned.

25
00:01:46,803 --> 00:01:49,406
Every time I left prison,

26
00:01:49,406 --> 00:01:52,242
I swore I wouldn't go back.

27
00:01:52,242 --> 00:01:58,615
But I did. Over and over and over again.

28
00:01:58,615 --> 00:02:02,486
I remember one day, standing in front of a judge

29
00:02:02,486 --> 00:02:08,458
and asking that judge, "Is there any help for me?"

30
00:02:08,458 --> 00:02:14,564
And the judge sentenced me to prison one more time.

31
00:02:14,564 --> 00:02:17,200
No one cared.

32
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,203
No one was listening.

33
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:24,541
But when I walked into that room in 1997,

34
00:02:24,541 --> 00:02:27,277
I was ready to be heard.

35
00:02:27,277 --> 00:02:30,614
In the rooms of Alcoholics Anonymous,

36
00:02:30,614 --> 00:02:35,152
they listened.

37
00:02:35,152 --> 00:02:38,855
I took a deep breath and stood up,

38
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:44,761
and said, "Look what drugs and alcohol have done to me."

39
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:47,698
The immediate compassion,

40
00:02:47,698 --> 00:02:49,866
empathy and love

41
00:02:49,866 --> 00:02:53,503
that rolled off these complete strangers

42
00:02:53,503 --> 00:02:56,873
was enough to put a sizable dent

43
00:02:56,873 --> 00:03:00,510
in my pain, my shame,

44
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:06,450
my guilt and all that sorrow.

45
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:09,152
I kept talking,

46
00:03:09,152 --> 00:03:12,155
and they kept listening.

47
00:03:12,155 --> 00:03:15,125
I shared all the thoughts

48
00:03:15,125 --> 00:03:20,030
I had spent decades trying to silence.

49
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:24,468
As they listened, I began to heal.

50
00:03:24,468 --> 00:03:29,773
Listening saved my life, and it opened a door.

51
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:33,009
I got sober in Santa Monica,

52
00:03:33,009 --> 00:03:37,047
an affluent part of Los Angeles.

53
00:03:37,047 --> 00:03:40,250
And I noticed something there.

54
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:43,253
People in Santa Monica

55
00:03:43,253 --> 00:03:46,656
weren’t going to prison for substance use,

56
00:03:46,656 --> 00:03:49,960
for alcoholism.

57
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:53,196
And I thought,

58
00:03:53,196 --> 00:03:56,166
why didn't that judge give me a paper and say,

59
00:03:56,166 --> 00:03:58,702
"Miss Burton,

60
00:03:58,702 --> 00:04:02,506
go to AA.”

61
00:04:02,506 --> 00:04:06,243
Was I not worth a piece of paper?

62
00:04:06,243 --> 00:04:11,715
Was I not worth direction toward a solution?

63
00:04:11,715 --> 00:04:15,452
So as I got stronger in my recovery,

64
00:04:15,452 --> 00:04:19,356
I thought about women just like me,

65
00:04:19,356 --> 00:04:23,360
cycling in and out of prison,

66
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:26,930
looking for safety and not finding it.

67
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:30,367
So I got a job.

68
00:04:30,367 --> 00:04:33,837
A minimum wage job.

69
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:38,041
And I saved $13,000.

70
00:04:38,041 --> 00:04:43,680
And I took $3,000 of it and put down on a house.

71
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:46,983
And I bought some furniture.

72
00:04:46,983 --> 00:04:48,385
Every morning I’d wake up,

73
00:04:48,385 --> 00:04:50,554
and I'd go down to the Greyhound bus station

74
00:04:50,554 --> 00:04:54,024
where women leaving prison were dropped off.

75
00:04:54,024 --> 00:04:56,860
And I knew that station well

76
00:04:56,860 --> 00:05:01,498
because I had been dropped off there so many times myself.

77
00:05:01,498 --> 00:05:05,869
And as they got off the bus carrying a trash bag of belongings

78
00:05:05,869 --> 00:05:09,005
and hope in their hearts,

79
00:05:09,005 --> 00:05:13,577
I would walk up to them and I'd say, "Girl, I have a house.

80
00:05:13,577 --> 00:05:16,546
And if you want to come live there,

81
00:05:16,546 --> 00:05:19,282
it's a safe place to be.

82
00:05:19,282 --> 00:05:21,985
You can come live there."

83
00:05:21,985 --> 00:05:27,357
And before long there were 11 of us living in that house.

84
00:05:27,357 --> 00:05:29,359
We cooked together.

85
00:05:29,359 --> 00:05:31,828
We went to meetings together.

86
00:05:31,828 --> 00:05:35,031
We shared resources.

87
00:05:35,031 --> 00:05:36,900
We laughed together.

88
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:39,202
We cried together.

89
00:05:39,202 --> 00:05:44,107
But most of all, we healed together.

90
00:05:44,107 --> 00:05:48,745
Before long, people saw what I was doing, and they joined me.

91
00:05:48,745 --> 00:05:51,781
And we created a team.

92
00:05:51,781 --> 00:05:56,887
Today, there [are] 12 houses across Los Angeles.

93
00:05:56,887 --> 00:05:59,856
(Applause)

94
00:05:59,856 --> 00:06:04,494
Thank you.

95
00:06:04,494 --> 00:06:10,467
What we built was a house of women helping women.

96
00:06:10,467 --> 00:06:15,772
Each house is staffed to support its residents

97
00:06:15,772 --> 00:06:18,208
who pay rent,

98
00:06:18,208 --> 00:06:20,377
do chores,

99
00:06:20,377 --> 00:06:22,412
buy groceries

100
00:06:22,412 --> 00:06:27,817
and learn to be accountable, responsible members of the community.

101
00:06:27,817 --> 00:06:31,154
We help them find jobs,

102
00:06:31,154 --> 00:06:34,257
learn financial literacy

103
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:38,094
and help them with any legal issues.

104
00:06:38,094 --> 00:06:43,400
As a result, thousands of women have come back to the community

105
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:50,040
and hundreds of mothers have been reunited with their children.

106
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:53,710
In 2017, I published my memoir,

107
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:55,912
"Becoming Ms. Burton:

108
00:06:55,912 --> 00:06:58,214
From Prison to Recovery

109
00:06:58,214 --> 00:07:02,118
to Leading the Fight for Incarcerated Women."

110
00:07:02,118 --> 00:07:07,791
In order to tell my story, I held book talks all over the nation,

111
00:07:07,791 --> 00:07:10,627
behind prison walls.

112
00:07:10,627 --> 00:07:15,065
There I would listen to women.

113
00:07:15,065 --> 00:07:19,569
They were pleading for a place to go after they were released.

114
00:07:19,569 --> 00:07:24,341
They were sitting with tears streaming down their faces,

115
00:07:24,341 --> 00:07:27,110
wanting to rebuild their lives,

116
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:30,046
be reunited with their children,

117
00:07:30,046 --> 00:07:33,750
find a job in their community [and]

118
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:38,455
break free from the criminal justice system.

119
00:07:38,455 --> 00:07:41,791
Every year in this country,

120
00:07:41,791 --> 00:07:46,763
around two million women are released from prisons and jails.

121
00:07:46,763 --> 00:07:50,633
They walk out with a trash bag of belongings,

122
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:53,303
hoping someone will listen.

123
00:07:53,303 --> 00:07:56,906
Hoping someone will help.

124
00:07:56,906 --> 00:07:58,575
When there's no help,

125
00:07:58,575 --> 00:08:01,578
when there's no safe place to land,

126
00:08:01,578 --> 00:08:05,315
too many of them will go back.

127
00:08:05,315 --> 00:08:11,888
I knew I could not provide resources of a new way of life

128
00:08:11,888 --> 00:08:15,358
to all those women.

129
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:17,994
So I created SAFE,

130
00:08:17,994 --> 00:08:22,966
Sisterhood Alliance for Freedom and Equality,

131
00:08:22,966 --> 00:08:25,402
to replicate my model.

132
00:08:25,402 --> 00:08:30,573
Our first training was with 13 women.

133
00:08:30,573 --> 00:08:33,243
To date, we've trained over 700.

134
00:08:33,243 --> 00:08:39,783
(Applause)

135
00:08:39,783 --> 00:08:41,785
To listen.

136
00:08:41,785 --> 00:08:44,120
To create support.

137
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:47,290
To give hope.

138
00:08:47,290 --> 00:08:52,328
When women like me have safe housing,

139
00:08:52,328 --> 00:08:54,664
families reunify,

140
00:08:54,664 --> 00:08:57,033
recidivism drops

141
00:08:57,033 --> 00:09:01,037
and communities stabilize.

142
00:09:01,037 --> 00:09:05,742
I started with one house of women helping women.

143
00:09:05,742 --> 00:09:10,713
Today, there is an entire network of women helping women

144
00:09:10,713 --> 00:09:15,151
in 20 US states and also in Uganda,

145
00:09:15,151 --> 00:09:18,121
Kenya and Nigeria.

146
00:09:18,121 --> 00:09:25,095
(Applause)

147
00:09:25,095 --> 00:09:29,632
We have replication down to a science.

148
00:09:29,632 --> 00:09:32,836
The leadership is there, the demand is there,

149
00:09:32,836 --> 00:09:37,173
and the goal is clear.

150
00:09:37,173 --> 00:09:41,111
More houses in more cities.

151
00:09:41,111 --> 00:09:45,448
More mothers reunited with their children.

152
00:09:45,448 --> 00:09:49,219
More women breaking a cycle

153
00:09:49,219 --> 00:09:53,123
that has lasted generations.

154
00:09:53,123 --> 00:09:59,696
I believe we all share a responsibility

155
00:09:59,696 --> 00:10:05,869
to heal the harms caused by the criminal justice system.

156
00:10:05,869 --> 00:10:09,038
This is public safety.

157
00:10:09,038 --> 00:10:12,275
This is family preservation.

158
00:10:12,275 --> 00:10:15,945
This is community repair.

159
00:10:15,945 --> 00:10:18,748
When someone listened to me

160
00:10:18,748 --> 00:10:22,719
with compassion rather than judgment,

161
00:10:22,719 --> 00:10:26,189
it changed my life.

162
00:10:26,189 --> 00:10:31,861
My story since that day in 1997,

163
00:10:31,861 --> 00:10:35,031
shows what is possible

164
00:10:35,031 --> 00:10:39,202
when people have support,

165
00:10:39,202 --> 00:10:41,070
hope

166
00:10:41,070 --> 00:10:45,375
and determination.

167
00:10:45,375 --> 00:10:50,079
I cannot bring my son back.

168
00:10:50,079 --> 00:10:57,253
But I know every time a woman walks through the doors and finds safety,

169
00:10:57,253 --> 00:11:02,192
every time a child is reunited with his mother,

170
00:11:02,192 --> 00:11:05,028
every time a woman breaks free

171
00:11:05,028 --> 00:11:08,398
from a cycle that once held me,

172
00:11:08,398 --> 00:11:13,536
his legacy goes way beyond his years.

173
00:11:13,536 --> 00:11:17,740
I believe he's smiling down on my efforts,

174
00:11:17,740 --> 00:11:21,277
and I hope he's proud of his mother.

175
00:11:21,277 --> 00:11:26,716
I am so grateful

176
00:11:26,716 --> 00:11:28,785
to be on the TED dot.

177
00:11:28,785 --> 00:11:31,054
(Laughter and Cheers)

178
00:11:31,054 --> 00:12:00,550
(Applause)

179
00:12:00,550 --> 00:12:06,055
And this happened because someone listened

180
00:12:06,055 --> 00:12:10,193
with compassion rather than judgment.

181
00:12:10,193 --> 00:12:11,628
Thank you so much.

182
00:12:11,628 --> 00:12:15,628
(Applause)
